WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.158
<v Lecturer>Okay, good morning, let's get going.

2
00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:08.208
<v Lecturer>Can the people at the back hear me?

3
00:00:08.783 --> 00:00:10.579
<v Lecturer>Wave if you can't.

4
00:00:11.363 --> 00:00:16.643
<v Lecturer>Right, nobody is waving, which either means you can hear me or you

5
00:00:16.703 --> 00:00:18.510
<v Lecturer>have given up already.

6
00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:21.210
<v Lecturer>Um, two quick notices.

7
00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:26.927
<v Lecturer>The tutorial problem for week five is now on the course page,

8
00:00:26.987 --> 00:00:31.988
<v Lecturer>and the reading for today is chapter three of the textbook,

9
00:00:32.048 --> 00:00:33.805
<v Lecturer>the part on agreement.

10
00:00:34.702 --> 00:00:38.402
<v Lecturer>If you have not done it, do it tonight,

11
00:00:38.462 --> 00:00:40.889
<v Lecturer>because the tutorial assumes you have.

12
00:00:43.514 --> 00:00:47.534
<v Lecturer>So. Last week we asked what a contract is,

13
00:00:47.594 --> 00:00:53.890
<v Lecturer>and the short answer was that it is an agreement the law will

14
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.421
<v Lecturer>enforce. Today we take the first half of that seriously.

15
00:00:59.181 --> 00:01:04.209
<v Lecturer>How does the law decide that two people have actually agreed?

16
00:01:04.696 --> 00:01:10.241
<v Lecturer>And the tool it uses, for almost every case you will meet this

17
00:01:10.301 --> 00:01:14.034
<v Lecturer>year, is a pair of ideas: offer and acceptance.

18
00:01:14.525 --> 00:01:19.253
<v Lecturer>One side makes an offer, the other side accepts it,

19
00:01:19.313 --> 00:01:23.760
<v Lecturer>and at the moment of acceptance, not a second before,

20
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.722
<v Lecturer>there is a contract.

21
00:01:29.593 --> 00:01:35.454
<v Lecturer>Before we get to the rules, one idea sits underneath all of them.

22
00:01:36.312 --> 00:01:38.563
<v Lecturer>The law judges agreement objectively.

23
00:01:39.203 --> 00:01:44.196
<v Lecturer>It does not ask what was really going on inside your head.

24
00:01:44.736 --> 00:01:50.601
<v Lecturer>It asks what a reasonable person, watching what you said and did,

25
00:01:50.661 --> 00:01:53.399
<v Lecturer>would have understood you to mean.

26
00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:58.051
<v Lecturer>Courts have said this for a very long time,

27
00:01:58.111 --> 00:02:03.951
<v Lecturer>and Smith v Hughes in 1871 is the case usually given for it.

28
00:02:06.136 --> 00:02:08.371
<v Lecturer>Why do it that way?

29
00:02:09.191 --> 00:02:12.862
<v Lecturer>Because nobody can see inside anybody else's head.

30
00:02:13.577 --> 00:02:18.321
<v Lecturer>If I could get out of a deal by saying,

31
00:02:18.381 --> 00:02:22.378
<v Lecturer>well, I said yes, but secretly I meant no,

32
00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:24.811
<v Lecturer>no contract would be safe.

33
00:02:25.396 --> 00:02:31.061
<v Lecturer>So, if you behave in a way that would lead a reasonable person

34
00:02:31.121 --> 00:02:36.826
<v Lecturer>to think you were agreeing, and the other side relies on that,

35
00:02:36.886 --> 00:02:39.309
<v Lecturer>you are held to it.

36
00:02:39.994 --> 00:02:44.569
<v Lecturer>Keep that in your mind for the rest of the hour.

37
00:02:45.455 --> 00:02:49.267
<v Lecturer>Almost every puzzle today comes back to the question,

38
00:02:49.327 --> 00:02:54.090
<v Lecturer>how would this look to a reasonable person standing outside?

39
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.751
<v Lecturer>So, what is an offer?

40
00:03:01.254 --> 00:03:07.608
<v Lecturer>An offer is a statement of the terms on which you are willing

41
00:03:07.668 --> 00:03:13.098
<v Lecturer>to be bound, made with the intention that it becomes binding as soon

42
00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:15.243
<v Lecturer>as the other person accepts.

43
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.734
<v Lecturer>The test is that last part.

44
00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:21.191
<v Lecturer>Has this person finished negotiating?

45
00:03:21.616 --> 00:03:26.083
<v Lecturer>If all the other side has to do is say yes,

46
00:03:26.143 --> 00:03:30.581
<v Lecturer>and a contract is made, then it is an offer.

47
00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:39.066
<v Lecturer>And the thing an offer has to be told apart from is called

48
00:03:39.126 --> 00:03:41.061
<v Lecturer>an invitation to treat.

49
00:03:41.901 --> 00:03:46.856
<v Lecturer>An invitation to treat is an invitation to make an offer.

50
00:03:47.421 --> 00:03:49.517
<v Lecturer>It is an opening move.

51
00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:55.259
<v Lecturer>It says, I might be interested, come and talk to me.

52
00:03:56.158 --> 00:04:00.986
<v Lecturer>It is not a promise to deal with anyone who turns up.

53
00:04:01.692 --> 00:04:07.209
<v Lecturer>And a great deal of the case law in this area is the

54
00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:11.043
<v Lecturer>courts sorting statements into one box or the other.

55
00:04:15.386 --> 00:04:21.652
<v Lecturer>Let's go through the usual examples, because these are the classic ones and

56
00:04:21.712 --> 00:04:24.930
<v Lecturer>you will see them again and again.

57
00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.487
<v Lecturer>First, goods on display in a shop.

58
00:04:28.905 --> 00:04:30.683
<v Lecturer>Second, advertisements. Third, auctions.

59
00:04:33.362 --> 00:04:35.211
<v Lecturer>Goods on display first.

60
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.526
<v Lecturer>In Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain v Boots,

61
00:04:39.586 --> 00:04:45.077
<v Lecturer>in 1953, the question was about one of the first self-service chemists.

62
00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:51.880
<v Lecturer>The customer takes the medicine off the shelf and carries it to the

63
00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.869
<v Lecturer>till, where a pharmacist is standing.

64
00:04:55.667 --> 00:05:02.023
<v Lecturer>The law said that certain medicines could only be sold under a pharmacist's

65
00:05:02.083 --> 00:05:05.787
<v Lecturer>supervision, so it mattered where the sale took place.

66
00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:12.831
<v Lecturer>The Court of Appeal held that the display on the shelf was only

67
00:05:12.891 --> 00:05:14.554
<v Lecturer>an invitation to treat.

68
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.529
<v Lecturer>The customer makes the offer, at the till,

69
00:05:18.589 --> 00:05:23.449
<v Lecturer>and the shop accepts it there, under the pharmacist's eye.

70
00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.647
<v Lecturer>So no offence.

71
00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.338
<v Lecturer>There is a good practical reason for that.

72
00:05:31.782 --> 00:05:37.215
<v Lecturer>If the display were the offer, then picking a box up would be

73
00:05:37.275 --> 00:05:42.819
<v Lecturer>acceptance, and you could not change your mind and put it back.

74
00:05:43.523 --> 00:05:47.706
<v Lecturer>And the shop could not refuse to sell to you.

75
00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:54.729
<v Lecturer>Treating the display as an invitation keeps both sides free until the till.

76
00:05:56.804 --> 00:06:02.395
<v Lecturer>The same idea was applied to a shop window in Fisher v Bell,

77
00:06:02.455 --> 00:06:07.708
<v Lecturer>in 1961. A shopkeeper had a flick knife in his window with a

78
00:06:07.768 --> 00:06:13.744
<v Lecturer>price ticket on it, and he was charged under a statute that made

79
00:06:13.804 --> 00:06:17.990
<v Lecturer>it an offence to offer such knives for sale.

80
00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:21.850
<v Lecturer>He was acquitted. In the law of contract,

81
00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:26.764
<v Lecturer>putting goods in the window is an invitation to treat,

82
00:06:26.824 --> 00:06:30.943
<v Lecturer>not an offer, and Parliament had used the word offer.

83
00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:37.493
<v Lecturer>Parliament changed the statute afterwards, which tells you something about how the judges

84
00:06:37.553 --> 00:06:38.377
<v Lecturer>read it.

85
00:06:41.071 --> 00:06:47.061
<v Lecturer>Advertisements next. The general rule is that an advertisement is an invitation to

86
00:06:47.121 --> 00:06:50.301
<v Lecturer>treat as well. In Partridge v Crittenden,

87
00:06:50.361 --> 00:06:55.611
<v Lecturer>in 1968, a man placed an advert in a magazine for some wild

88
00:06:55.671 --> 00:07:00.072
<v Lecturer>birds, and he was charged with offering them for sale.

89
00:07:00.485 --> 00:07:06.519
<v Lecturer>Again, no offence. An advert in a magazine is an invitation to treat.

90
00:07:06.996 --> 00:07:12.532
<v Lecturer>And the reason the court gave is the one you should remember:

91
00:07:12.592 --> 00:07:18.366
<v Lecturer>if an advert were an offer, the seller would be bound to sell

92
00:07:18.426 --> 00:07:24.583
<v Lecturer>to every single person who replied, and he only had so many birds.

93
00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:30.284
<v Lecturer>And auctions. When the auctioneer asks for bids,

94
00:07:30.344 --> 00:07:33.207
<v Lecturer>that is an invitation to treat.

95
00:07:33.972 --> 00:07:36.380
<v Lecturer>Each bid is an offer.

96
00:07:36.845 --> 00:07:42.283
<v Lecturer>And the auctioneer accepts one of them by bringing the hammer down.

97
00:07:42.794 --> 00:07:47.497
<v Lecturer>Until the hammer falls, a bidder can withdraw the bid.

98
00:07:50.096 --> 00:07:53.343
<v Student>Sorry, so is an advert never an offer?

99
00:07:55.723 --> 00:07:58.409
<v Lecturer>Good question, and no, not never.

100
00:07:59.055 --> 00:08:02.022
<v Lecturer>That is exactly where we go next.

101
00:08:02.792 --> 00:08:06.501
<v Lecturer>The rule about adverts is only a starting point,

102
00:08:06.561 --> 00:08:12.674
<v Lecturer>and the most famous contract case of all is about an advert that

103
00:08:12.734 --> 00:08:14.120
<v Lecturer>was an offer.

104
00:08:18.273 --> 00:08:21.331
<v Lecturer>Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball Company, 1893.

105
00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.423
<v Lecturer>If you remember one case from this course,

106
00:08:25.483 --> 00:08:28.261
<v Lecturer>it will probably be this one.

107
00:08:28.758 --> 00:08:32.368
<v Lecturer>The company sold a device, a smoke ball,

108
00:08:32.428 --> 00:08:36.098
<v Lecturer>that you were meant to sniff to prevent influenza.

109
00:08:36.792 --> 00:08:42.233
<v Lecturer>And it ran an advertisement saying it would pay one hundred pounds to

110
00:08:42.293 --> 00:08:47.175
<v Lecturer>anyone who used the ball three times a day for two weeks,

111
00:08:47.235 --> 00:08:49.677
<v Lecturer>as directed, and still caught influenza.

112
00:08:50.475 --> 00:08:56.688
<v Lecturer>And to show it was serious, the advert said the company had deposited

113
00:08:56.748 --> 00:09:00.572
<v Lecturer>a thousand pounds with its bank for the purpose.

114
00:09:02.988 --> 00:09:04.689
<v Lecturer>Mrs Carlill bought one.

115
00:09:05.367 --> 00:09:07.788
<v Lecturer>She used it as directed.

116
00:09:08.493 --> 00:09:12.418
<v Lecturer>She caught influenza. She asked for her hundred pounds,

117
00:09:12.478 --> 00:09:18.052
<v Lecturer>and the company said, in effect, we never made you a promise,

118
00:09:18.112 --> 00:09:20.223
<v Lecturer>that was only an advertisement.

119
00:09:21.117 --> 00:09:23.432
<v Lecturer>The Court of Appeal disagreed.

120
00:09:23.975 --> 00:09:27.457
<v Lecturer>It held that this advert was an offer,

121
00:09:27.517 --> 00:09:33.122
<v Lecturer>made to the whole world, which anyone could accept by doing what it

122
00:09:33.182 --> 00:09:33.667
<v Lecturer>asked.

123
00:09:35.487 --> 00:09:39.219
<v Lecturer>Now, the company ran a whole list of arguments,

124
00:09:39.279 --> 00:09:43.848
<v Lecturer>and the court's answers to them are what you need.

125
00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.935
<v Lecturer>The company said the advert was just a sales puff,

126
00:09:48.995 --> 00:09:53.314
<v Lecturer>not meant seriously. The court said, look at the deposit:

127
00:09:53.374 --> 00:09:58.606
<v Lecturer>you told people you had put a thousand pounds in the bank to

128
00:09:58.666 --> 00:10:02.921
<v Lecturer>back the promise. A reasonable reader would take that seriously.

129
00:10:03.455 --> 00:10:08.935
<v Lecturer>The company said you cannot make an offer to the whole world.

130
00:10:09.793 --> 00:10:15.261
<v Lecturer>The court said you can, and it is accepted by whoever does the

131
00:10:15.321 --> 00:10:21.340
<v Lecturer>thing asked. And the company said, she never told us she was accepting.

132
00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:26.322
<v Lecturer>The court said that in an offer of this kind,

133
00:10:26.382 --> 00:10:29.325
<v Lecturer>the offeror has, by its own words,

134
00:10:29.385 --> 00:10:32.859
<v Lecturer>done away with the need to be told.

135
00:10:33.336 --> 00:10:36.480
<v Lecturer>Doing what was asked is the acceptance.

136
00:10:38.832 --> 00:10:43.163
<v Lecturer>An offer like that is called a unilateral offer.

137
00:10:43.945 --> 00:10:48.415
<v Lecturer>Only one side makes a promise: if you do this,

138
00:10:48.475 --> 00:10:50.409
<v Lecturer>I will pay you.

139
00:10:51.134 --> 00:10:53.374
<v Lecturer>The other side promises nothing.

140
00:10:53.899 --> 00:10:59.174
<v Lecturer>They just perform. Rewards for lost pets are the everyday example.

141
00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:03.067
<v Lecturer>Compare that with the ordinary bilateral contract,

142
00:11:03.127 --> 00:11:05.956
<v Lecturer>where there are promises on both sides:

143
00:11:06.016 --> 00:11:09.759
<v Lecturer>I promise to sell, you promise to pay.

144
00:11:11.779 --> 00:11:15.163
<v Lecturer>And this will be on the exam,

145
00:11:15.223 --> 00:11:17.535
<v Lecturer>I am telling you now.

146
00:11:18.285 --> 00:11:22.927
<v Lecturer>Every year a problem question has an advertisement in it,

147
00:11:22.987 --> 00:11:27.677
<v Lecturer>and every year some people write, adverts are invitations to treat,

148
00:11:27.737 --> 00:11:31.299
<v Lecturer>end of story. That will lose you marks.

149
00:11:31.977 --> 00:11:35.751
<v Lecturer>The right answer works through it: the general rule,

150
00:11:35.811 --> 00:11:38.750
<v Lecturer>then whether this advert is like Carlill.

151
00:11:39.271 --> 00:11:41.353
<v Lecturer>Is it clear and definite?

152
00:11:42.229 --> 00:11:46.841
<v Lecturer>Does it ask for an act rather than for a reply?

153
00:11:47.494 --> 00:11:52.878
<v Lecturer>Is there anything, like the deposit, that shows it was meant to be

154
00:11:52.938 --> 00:11:57.435
<v Lecturer>taken seriously? That is the answer the marker is looking for.

155
00:12:02.202 --> 00:12:05.621
<v Lecturer>Right. So someone has made an offer.

156
00:12:06.344 --> 00:12:08.736
<v Lecturer>What counts as accepting it?

157
00:12:09.216 --> 00:12:14.788
<v Lecturer>The basic rule is that acceptance has to match the offer exactly.

158
00:12:15.428 --> 00:12:18.635
<v Lecturer>People call this the mirror image rule.

159
00:12:19.522 --> 00:12:22.843
<v Lecturer>The acceptance is a mirror of the offer.

160
00:12:23.363 --> 00:12:25.766
<v Lecturer>Yes, on exactly those terms.

161
00:12:27.323 --> 00:12:33.458
<v Lecturer>If you reply with different terms, that is not an acceptance at all.

162
00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:35.636
<v Lecturer>It is a counter-offer.

163
00:12:36.178 --> 00:12:38.994
<v Lecturer>And a counter-offer does two things.

164
00:12:39.788 --> 00:12:45.592
<v Lecturer>It is a new offer, which the other side can now accept or

165
00:12:45.652 --> 00:12:48.860
<v Lecturer>reject. And it kills the original offer.

166
00:12:49.659 --> 00:12:52.201
<v Lecturer>You cannot go back to it.

167
00:12:54.291 --> 00:12:57.269
<v Lecturer>The case is Hyde v Wrench, 1840.

168
00:12:58.128 --> 00:13:03.696
<v Lecturer>Wrench offered to sell his farm to Hyde for a thousand pounds.

169
00:13:04.479 --> 00:13:07.475
<v Lecturer>Hyde replied offering nine hundred and fifty.

170
00:13:08.372 --> 00:13:11.609
<v Lecturer>Wrench said no. So Hyde then wrote,

171
00:13:11.669 --> 00:13:13.873
<v Lecturer>fine, I accept the thousand.

172
00:13:14.328 --> 00:13:17.913
<v Lecturer>And the court held there was no contract.

173
00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:23.573
<v Lecturer>The offer of a thousand had died when Hyde made his counter-offer,

174
00:13:23.633 --> 00:13:27.821
<v Lecturer>and there was nothing left for him to accept.

175
00:13:30.477 --> 00:13:36.686
<v Lecturer>But be careful with that, because not every reply that is not a

176
00:13:36.746 --> 00:13:38.877
<v Lecturer>plain yes is a counter-offer.

177
00:13:39.524 --> 00:13:44.826
<v Lecturer>In Stevenson v McLean, in 1880, the buyer replied to an offer to

178
00:13:44.886 --> 00:13:50.450
<v Lecturer>sell iron by asking whether he could take delivery over a few months.

179
00:13:51.353 --> 00:13:55.484
<v Lecturer>That was held to be only a request for information.

180
00:13:56.179 --> 00:14:01.691
<v Lecturer>It did not reject anything, so the offer was still there to be

181
00:14:01.751 --> 00:14:07.244
<v Lecturer>accepted. So the question is always, did this reply turn the offer down

182
00:14:07.304 --> 00:14:10.472
<v Lecturer>and put new terms in its place,

183
00:14:10.532 --> 00:14:12.575
<v Lecturer>or was it just asking?

184
00:14:15.034 --> 00:14:20.845
<v Lecturer>And in business, the mirror image rule leads to something called the battle

185
00:14:20.905 --> 00:14:25.462
<v Lecturer>of the forms. Each company sends its own standard printed terms,

186
00:14:25.522 --> 00:14:30.742
<v Lecturer>the buyer on its order form, the seller on its acknowledgement,

187
00:14:30.802 --> 00:14:33.292
<v Lecturer>and the two sets never match.

188
00:14:33.773 --> 00:14:37.485
<v Lecturer>In Butler Machine Tool v Ex-Cell-O, in 1979,

189
00:14:37.545 --> 00:14:42.869
<v Lecturer>the Court of Appeal mostly went with what is called the last shot.

190
00:14:43.689 --> 00:14:48.189
<v Lecturer>The last set of terms sent, and then acted on,

191
00:14:48.249 --> 00:14:53.853
<v Lecturer>usually wins. On those facts, the sellers had signed and returned the buyer's

192
00:14:53.913 --> 00:14:58.825
<v Lecturer>own tear-off slip, so the contract was on the buyer's terms.

193
00:14:59.584 --> 00:15:03.987
<v Lecturer>You do not need the detail for this week.

194
00:15:04.526 --> 00:15:09.809
<v Lecturer>Just know the phrase, and that it comes straight out of the mirror

195
00:15:09.869 --> 00:15:10.732
<v Lecturer>image rule.

196
00:15:15.227 --> 00:15:19.097
<v Lecturer>Next. An acceptance generally has to be communicated.

197
00:15:19.831 --> 00:15:22.943
<v Lecturer>It is not enough to decide, privately,

198
00:15:23.003 --> 00:15:27.084
<v Lecturer>that you accept. The offeror has to be told.

199
00:15:27.605 --> 00:15:32.958
<v Lecturer>And the flip side of that is that silence is not acceptance.

200
00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:38.381
<v Lecturer>The case is Felthouse v Bindley, 1862,

201
00:15:38.441 --> 00:15:42.161
<v Lecturer>which is a lovely case about a horse.

202
00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:46.486
<v Lecturer>An uncle was negotiating to buy his nephew's horse.

203
00:15:46.928 --> 00:15:51.288
<v Lecturer>He wrote to the nephew saying, more or less,

204
00:15:51.348 --> 00:15:54.221
<v Lecturer>if I hear nothing more about it,

205
00:15:54.281 --> 00:15:57.999
<v Lecturer>I will consider the horse mine at that price.

206
00:15:58.704 --> 00:16:00.428
<v Lecturer>The nephew never replied.

207
00:16:00.844 --> 00:16:06.961
<v Lecturer>He did tell the auctioneer who was selling his stock to keep the

208
00:16:07.021 --> 00:16:11.249
<v Lecturer>horse back, but the auctioneer sold it by mistake.

209
00:16:11.923 --> 00:16:17.275
<v Lecturer>And the uncle sued the auctioneer, saying the horse was already his.

210
00:16:17.822 --> 00:16:19.765
<v Lecturer>The court said no.

211
00:16:20.437 --> 00:16:24.814
<v Lecturer>The nephew had never communicated an acceptance to the uncle,

212
00:16:24.874 --> 00:16:30.726
<v Lecturer>and you cannot make somebody's silence into a yes just by saying so

213
00:16:30.786 --> 00:16:32.222
<v Lecturer>in your offer.

214
00:16:34.462 --> 00:16:37.685
<v Lecturer>There is a sensible policy there as well.

215
00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:43.821
<v Lecturer>Nobody should be able to force a contract on you by writing,

216
00:16:43.881 --> 00:16:47.712
<v Lecturer>if you do not reply, you have agreed.

217
00:16:48.589 --> 00:16:51.939
<v Lecturer>Think about what unsolicited post would turn into.

218
00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:56.072
<v Lecturer>Now, when is acceptance communicated?

219
00:16:56.518 --> 00:16:59.912
<v Lecturer>With instant ways of talking, face to face,

220
00:16:59.972 --> 00:17:04.622
<v Lecturer>by telephone, the courts said in Entores v Miles Far East,

221
00:17:04.682 --> 00:17:09.005
<v Lecturer>1955, that the acceptance counts when it is received.

222
00:17:09.531 --> 00:17:15.431
<v Lecturer>That case was about telex, which is a kind of very old instant

223
00:17:15.491 --> 00:17:19.596
<v Lecturer>message, and the point was where the contract was made.

224
00:17:20.095 --> 00:17:23.515
<v Lecturer>It was made where the acceptance arrived,

225
00:17:23.575 --> 00:17:24.481
<v Lecturer>in London.

226
00:17:27.157 --> 00:17:32.622
<v Lecturer>But there is one big exception, and it is the postal rule.

227
00:17:33.334 --> 00:17:36.687
<v Lecturer>Where it is reasonable to accept by post,

228
00:17:36.747 --> 00:17:41.899
<v Lecturer>the acceptance is complete as soon as the letter is posted,

229
00:17:41.959 --> 00:17:43.602
<v Lecturer>not when it arrives.

230
00:17:44.093 --> 00:17:47.797
<v Lecturer>That comes from Adams v Lindsell, in 1818.

231
00:17:48.299 --> 00:17:53.692
<v Lecturer>And it means the contract can exist even if the letter is delayed,

232
00:17:53.752 --> 00:17:58.704
<v Lecturer>or even if it is lost and never arrives at all,

233
00:17:58.764 --> 00:18:03.564
<v Lecturer>which is what happened in Household Fire Insurance v Grant,

234
00:18:03.624 --> 00:18:04.093
<v Lecturer>1879.

235
00:18:05.983 --> 00:18:10.181
<v Lecturer>So why on earth would the law do that?

236
00:18:10.649 --> 00:18:14.690
<v Lecturer>Well, someone has to carry the risk of the post,

237
00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:20.620
<v Lecturer>and the courts put it on the person who made the offer,

238
00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:26.635
<v Lecturer>because they chose to start the deal in a way that invited a

239
00:18:26.695 --> 00:18:30.281
<v Lecturer>reply by post. And they can protect themselves.

240
00:18:30.737 --> 00:18:36.070
<v Lecturer>If the offer says, I must actually receive your acceptance in writing,

241
00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:39.163
<v Lecturer>then the postal rule does not apply.

242
00:18:39.786 --> 00:18:42.790
<v Lecturer>That was Holwell Securities v Hughes, 1974.

243
00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:47.922
<v Student>Does the postal rule work for email?

244
00:18:50.364 --> 00:18:56.630
<v Lecturer>That is the question everybody asks, and the honest answer is that it

245
00:18:56.690 --> 00:19:01.614
<v Lecturer>has never been settled cleanly by an appellate court in England.

246
00:19:02.434 --> 00:19:08.197
<v Lecturer>Most writers think email is closer to the instantaneous cases than to the

247
00:19:08.257 --> 00:19:11.806
<v Lecturer>post, so acceptance would count when it arrives,

248
00:19:11.866 --> 00:19:15.770
<v Lecturer>but you should say that it is uncertain,

249
00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:20.689
<v Lecturer>and argue it, rather than state it as a rule.

250
00:19:24.935 --> 00:19:27.436
<v Lecturer>Last topic before we sum up.

251
00:19:27.985 --> 00:19:32.387
<v Lecturer>An offer can come to an end before anyone accepts it,

252
00:19:32.447 --> 00:19:35.376
<v Lecturer>and then there is nothing to accept.

253
00:19:36.146 --> 00:19:39.446
<v Lecturer>There are four ways an offer can end.

254
00:19:40.199 --> 00:19:42.818
<v Lecturer>First, revocation: the offeror withdraws it.

255
00:19:43.622 --> 00:19:48.855
<v Lecturer>Second, rejection, which includes a counter-offer, as in Hyde v Wrench.

256
00:19:49.421 --> 00:19:54.022
<v Lecturer>Third, lapse of time: an offer does not stay open forever,

257
00:19:54.082 --> 00:19:57.125
<v Lecturer>it ends at the time it gives,

258
00:19:57.185 --> 00:20:01.565
<v Lecturer>or after a reasonable time if it gives none.

259
00:20:02.229 --> 00:20:05.255
<v Lecturer>And fourth, the death of a party,

260
00:20:05.315 --> 00:20:06.560
<v Lecturer>in some cases.

261
00:20:08.919 --> 00:20:11.967
<v Lecturer>Revocation is the one with the traps.

262
00:20:12.787 --> 00:20:16.525
<v Lecturer>An offeror can revoke at any time before acceptance,

263
00:20:16.585 --> 00:20:21.001
<v Lecturer>even if they said they would keep the offer open,

264
00:20:21.061 --> 00:20:25.140
<v Lecturer>unless the other side paid something to keep it open.

265
00:20:26.049 --> 00:20:30.385
<v Lecturer>But the revocation only works once it is communicated.

266
00:20:31.224 --> 00:20:35.772
<v Lecturer>And here the postal rule does not help the offeror.

267
00:20:38.651 --> 00:20:41.966
<v Lecturer>In Byrne v Van Tienhoven, in 1880,

268
00:20:42.026 --> 00:20:44.416
<v Lecturer>a company posted an offer.

269
00:20:45.205 --> 00:20:48.677
<v Lecturer>Then it posted a letter revoking the offer.

270
00:20:49.348 --> 00:20:54.642
<v Lecturer>Meanwhile, the buyers received the offer and accepted it at once,

271
00:20:54.702 --> 00:20:58.119
<v Lecturer>by telegram. The revocation letter arrived later.

272
00:20:58.956 --> 00:21:02.082
<v Lecturer>The court held there was a contract.

273
00:21:02.694 --> 00:21:07.329
<v Lecturer>A revocation counts only when it actually reaches the other side,

274
00:21:07.389 --> 00:21:10.610
<v Lecturer>and by then the offer had been accepted.

275
00:21:13.076 --> 00:21:18.439
<v Lecturer>And the revocation does not have to come from the offeror personally.

276
00:21:19.218 --> 00:21:25.523
<v Lecturer>In Dickinson v Dodds, in 1876, Dodds offered to sell some houses to

277
00:21:25.583 --> 00:21:30.130
<v Lecturer>Dickinson, and said the offer was open until a Friday.

278
00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:34.544
<v Lecturer>Before Friday, Dodds sold the houses to someone else,

279
00:21:34.604 --> 00:21:38.056
<v Lecturer>and Dickinson heard about it from another person,

280
00:21:38.116 --> 00:21:43.993
<v Lecturer>who was reliable. The court held that Dickinson knew the offer had been

281
00:21:44.053 --> 00:21:48.580
<v Lecturer>withdrawn just as surely as if Dodds had told him himself,

282
00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:52.567
<v Lecturer>so it was too late for him to accept.

283
00:21:56.604 --> 00:22:01.953
<v Lecturer>Okay. Let's pull it together, because I want you to leave with a

284
00:22:02.013 --> 00:22:05.272
<v Lecturer>method rather than a list of cases.

285
00:22:06.033 --> 00:22:11.173
<v Lecturer>When you get a problem question about whether there is a contract,

286
00:22:11.233 --> 00:22:14.041
<v Lecturer>ask four things, in this order.

287
00:22:16.217 --> 00:22:20.651
<v Lecturer>First, was there an offer, or only an invitation to treat?

288
00:22:21.174 --> 00:22:25.145
<v Lecturer>Think shop displays, adverts and auctions, and remember Carlill.

289
00:22:26.037 --> 00:22:29.750
<v Lecturer>Second, was there an acceptance that mirrors the offer,

290
00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:34.248
<v Lecturer>or was it a counter-offer, or just a request for information?

291
00:22:34.863 --> 00:22:38.337
<v Lecturer>Third, was the acceptance communicated, and if so,

292
00:22:38.397 --> 00:22:41.931
<v Lecturer>when? Here you need silence, the instantaneous cases,

293
00:22:41.991 --> 00:22:45.038
<v Lecturer>and the postal rule with its exceptions.

294
00:22:45.633 --> 00:22:50.056
<v Lecturer>And fourth, was the offer still open when it was accepted,

295
00:22:50.116 --> 00:22:53.500
<v Lecturer>or had it been revoked, rejected or lapsed?

296
00:22:55.102 --> 00:22:58.985
<v Lecturer>If you work through those four in order,

297
00:22:59.045 --> 00:23:02.816
<v Lecturer>and you apply the objective test as you go,

298
00:23:02.876 --> 00:23:08.590
<v Lecturer>you will get most problem questions in this part of the course right.

299
00:23:09.151 --> 00:23:14.619
<v Lecturer>Next week, consideration, which is the other half of what makes a promise

300
00:23:14.679 --> 00:23:17.613
<v Lecturer>binding. Read chapter four for that.

301
00:23:18.461 --> 00:23:21.774
<v Lecturer>Okay, thanks everyone, see you in the tutorials.
